    "description": "A request handler for a dashboard screen concurrently retrieves the user's profile from a database, recent orders from a billing service, and recommendations from a separate worker. The client may disconnect, the request may exceed its deadline, or one dependency may fail. In every case, the handler must stop waiting for unnecessary results, release database connections and sockets, and prevent abandoned child work from continuing in the background. The three dependencies support cooperative interruption but may perform cleanup before they stop, so immediate forced termination is unsafe. The handler also needs to distinguish an ordinary dependency failure from a request that was deliberately canceled, and it must not report success after the caller has gone away. The implementation should keep the stop signal separate from the returned business result, pass it through nested calls, and ensure that every component involved in the request observes it before the operation is considered finished.",
    "decision_statement": "How should the handler propagate one cooperative stop request through its dependent calls, clean up safely, and report cancellation separately from failure?",
    "notes": "cancellation-token fits the cooperative stop signal threaded through nested calls with cleanup and a distinct canceled outcome; structured-concurrency is the near miss for the parent-owned fan-out, with async-method-invocation supporting concurrent retrieval."
  },
